{
  "dom_id": "step-q97776",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(x-1)\\sqrt{4+2x-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+2x+4 & =-({x}^{2}-2x-4) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}-4) \\\\  & =-((x-1)^{2}-5) \\\\  & =5-(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(x-1)\\sqrt{4+2x-{x}^{2}}} & =\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(x-1)\\sqrt{5-{(x-1)}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}})\\sqrt{5-5{(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{(x-1)\\sqrt{4+2x-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\int \\frac{1}{(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}})\\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{\\sqrt{5}})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
